# Keep shell scripts and Unix-only files LF in the repo regardless of the
# checkout platform — they run in Linux/CI and CRLF would break the shebang.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf

# Source, headers, docs and schemas: force LF too, so the PC<->MacBook Syncthing
# sync can't flip line endings and produce whole-file CRLF diffs.
*.cpp       text eol=lf
*.cc        text eol=lf
*.c         text eol=lf
*.h         text eol=lf
*.hpp       text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
*.cmake     text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf

# ⚠️ NOT IN THE PUBLISHED SNAPSHOT. deploy/export-snapshot.sh reads this attribute and drops
# the paths it names, so anything listed here stays private. It reads it explicitly because
# the snapshot is built with `git read-tree`, and export-ignore is honoured by `git archive`
# alone — a tree built any other way carries the file unless something acts on the attribute.
#
# public_repo_hygiene.sh is the guard that keeps the agent working guides, lab hosts and
# people's names out of what gets published — and to do that it must spell out each string it
# forbids. A guard cannot refuse a word without containing it. Published, it would be the one
# file carrying those strings into a public repository, which is precisely what it exists to
# prevent. It stays here, where it runs on every gate, and run_all.sh knows an export-ignored
# suite is absent by design rather than missing by accident.
tests/public_repo_hygiene.sh export-ignore

# Our licence issuing tool: it signs with a key only we hold, so it is of no use in the
# published snapshot and its presence there would only invite confusion.
deploy/license-issue.sh export-ignore

# Our release key generator: it makes the private keys that authenticate our software.
deploy/release-keygen.sh export-ignore

# Our release signing step: it uses the private release keys, which stay on the maintainer's machine.
deploy/release-sign.sh export-ignore
